WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.373
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.373 --> 00:00:09.990
Sal Khan here from Kahn Academy.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.640
Welcome to our Daily Homeroom live stream

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.710
which is really just a way

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.950
of having interesting conversations

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:19.000
and staying connected during
this time of school closures

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.810
and social distancing.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.500
Before we get into what I
expect to be a very exciting

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.240
and interesting
conversation with our guest,

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.270
I will give my standard announcement.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:30.190
We are not for profit,
we're only able to exist

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:32.250
because of philanthropic donations.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.950
I wanna give a special shout
out to several corporations

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.110
that have stepped up
in the last few weeks,

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.140
especially as our costs have gone up.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.620
Our usage has been about 2.5, three X

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:44.130
of what it typically is
during this COVID crisis.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.380
You can imagine server costs,

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.860
and we're trying to increase our programs

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:49.140
and content for a lot of folks.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.710
So special thanks to Bank of America,

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:55.310
Google.org, Novartis, AT&amp;T, and Fastly,

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.650
and our many other supporters
who have been supporting

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:58.900
Kahn Academy through the years

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.440
that have really put us in a position

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.310
to be able to do the work we're doing now.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.070
We still are running at a deficit.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:06.300
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.140
please think about making
a donation to Kahn Academy.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.230
With that out of the way, I'm excited

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:13.890
to introduce our guest today,

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.230
David Leonhardt who's a writer
for the "New York Times".

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.890
Good to see you, David.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:20.723
- Good to see you, Sal.

00:01:20.723 --> 00:01:22.150
Thanks for having me.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:23.230
- No, it's an honor.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.100
And you know, you've been
writing for a little while

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.370
and you're doing a lot this summer

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:31.280
or the spring on inequality.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.913
So what got you interested in inequality?

00:01:36.488 --> 00:01:40.440
- I guess, my background
when I was in school,

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.143
my favorite subject was math,

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.510
but I also really liked working
on the student newspaper.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:49.060
So I think to some extent,
it's just a reflective

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.460
of this long-time interest
that I've always had

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.290
of describing the world
around me using numbers,

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.740
and then doing it for a general audience,

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.010
rather than an academic audience.

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:04.010
But then as I became a journalist
over the last 25 years,

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:08.010
I really came to think this was one

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:09.550
of the most important things,

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.080
really along with climate change,

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.200
going on in our country.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.560
The fact that the living
standards of most Americans

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.390
don't track anymore with
the statistics you read

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:24.000
on what our economy as a whole is doing.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.400
And that really creates
all kinds of problems.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.010
And in a way, that's
really what inequality is.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.690
And so I think it affects
everything from politics

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.270
to obviously, living standards,

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:36.080
to education, to health.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.560
We clearly see it playing out now

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.480
in the coronavirus.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.510
And so it's one of these
things in which everywhere

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:43.210
I looked around, or almost everywhere,

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:46.480
I saw inequality kind of as an explanation

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.763
for a lot of the huge
trends in our society.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.840
- And I wanna look at some
of the charts in a little bit

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.480
from some of your op-ed pieces,

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.090
and some of your articles you've written.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.530
But you know, just as a, maybe this will

00:02:58.530 --> 00:02:59.410
be a naive question,

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:00.530
but just as a level set.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.740
You know, in society,
some level of inequality

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.863
one might argue is inevitable.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.720
Is there a certain
threshold of when inequality

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.113
becomes too much?

00:03:12.177 --> 00:03:14.080
And you said that it
could lead to problems.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.780
What type of problems do you think

00:03:17.009 --> 00:03:20.090
large inequalities could lead to?

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.110
- Yeah, I think that's
an important level set.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:26.560
I mean, I think not only is
some degree of inequality

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.330
probably inevitable, but I
would go so far as to say

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:30.720
it's probably desirable.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.540
The societies that have tried

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.460
to get rid of inequality entirely

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.500
have failed, essentially.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.240
And probably the best,

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.470
most tangible modern example is in China

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.140
in which in the 1970s, China discovered

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:48.610
that it was really having a
hard time getting anything done

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:50.120
because they were insisting that everyone

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.790
was gonna have the same no matter what.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.593
People weren't farming their
lands in efficient ways.

00:03:55.685 --> 00:03:58.300
And the great sort of
economic revolution in China

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.540
was lead by Deng Xiaoping

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.940
in which he tried to move, essentially,

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.620
toward a system that was
going to inevitably result

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.300
in more inequality.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:10.550
But it also lifted almost
everyone's living standards.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.070
The problem is was when inequality

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:13.530
just gets too, too big.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.070
And we've seen this happen a number

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:16.460
of times throughout history.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:17.910
It happened in parts

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:19.810
of the late 19th century in this country.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:21.280
It happened in the 1920s.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:23.270
And when inequality gets too big,

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:24.760
you have many problems.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:25.900
But I'll highlight two.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:29.870
One is that the people who
are amassing huge amounts

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:32.590
of society's resources end up being able

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.970
to essentially set the rules

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:36.170
through the amount of influence

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:39.170
they have over both the
economy and politics

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.380
to make sure they never
lose what they have.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.320
And two, is you end up
with a lot of people

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:47.210
whose living standards just
aren't rising very rapidly

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:50.350
because you have such a large
share of society's resources

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:53.060
going to such a small portion of society.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.590
And you know, it's subjective
about when it's too much.

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:59.670
But when you look at just how
much inequality has increased

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.850
over the last 30, 40, 50
years in this society.

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.690
It's been so extreme

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:06.307
that I think you either have to say,

00:05:06.307 --> 00:05:09.347
"Well boy, something was
terribly wrong in our society

00:05:09.347 --> 00:05:11.817
"in the '50s and '60s
and it was too equal,

00:05:11.817 --> 00:05:14.440
"or something is wrong
now, and it's too unequal."

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:18.573
It's hard to really imagine
that both can work well.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:20.990
- And there's this fairness argument

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:22.210
which you make very well just now

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:25.240
that at some point, money
to some degree is power

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.510
and then that power could
be to keep more money.

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:30.070
And then obviously, there's a fairness

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:32.060
that it just gets at some point unfair.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:33.330
But are there other implications?

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.300
I mean, if it gets too unequal,

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.350
the things like the French Revolution,

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.050
or I mean, is it just the
nature of our democracy

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.093
become less stable?

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:43.173
- I think it does.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.470
There's a fascinating book

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:47.475
by a couple of political scientists

00:05:47.475 --> 00:05:50.960
in which they look at why nations fail.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:52.840
I believe that's actually
the title of the book.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.240
And they argue that over
the course of history,

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:58.160
inequality is the key, not the only,

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.020
but is the key reason
why societies decline.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.820
That when basically you have societies

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.550
in which the mass of
people are not enjoying

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.210
rising living standards, it can lead

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.150
to political instability.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:12.900
It also can end up being
a real waste of talent.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.370
If large sections of your society

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:17.190
are closed off from opportunity,

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.140
or even if they're not
completely closed off,

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:20.670
if opportunity's so difficult

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:23.280
that most of the people in those sections

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.310
of society will not be
allowed to escape it,

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.940
you end up missing out on
huge amounts of talent.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:32.250
There's a recent research
paper called "Lost Einsteins",

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:34.560
that tries to quantify some of the costs

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.000
of our rising inequality.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:39.410
And the idea is that there
are probably hundreds

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:43.200
of kids today who might
reach the very, very top

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.150
of science and other fields

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:48.050
if only they had better opportunities

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:48.920
than they do.

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:51.960
And so there really are,
even if you can't predict

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.591
when those bills will come due,

00:06:54.591 --> 00:06:57.180
or exactly how they'll come due,

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.040
there really are long-term costs.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:01.920
And I think even people
who are on the happy end

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.120
on many of the inequality trends

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:06.400
should have some reason to worry

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.570
that in the long-term, it
could lead to a society

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:11.890
that functions much less helpfully

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:16.190
than one where living
standards are rising broadly

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:18.393
for most of the population.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.450
- And let's make that a
little bit more tangible.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.690
Philippe, if we could
bring up that first chart

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.370
which comes from one of David's articles

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.160
that shows inequality, or actually,

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:31.550
it shows per capita GDP over
the last several decades,

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:34.100
and how the different groups.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:36.030
David, could you just
explain what's going on here?

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:36.910
It might be a little bit small

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:39.610
depending on how large
people's screens are,

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:41.420
but what do we see here?

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:44.253
- Yeah, so I love this chart.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:47.530
And so I think the place
to start is exactly

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:48.690
where you said we should start

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.230
which is per capita GDP.

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:52.620
So that's that dark black line

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:55.600
with per capita GDP pointing at it.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:58.650
So that's basically over, since 1980,

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:01.100
that's the cumulative percentage change

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.380
in how much our economy
produces every year.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:06.520
So you take the total value
of everything we've produced,

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:09.903
all the services, all
the goods, everything,

00:08:10.919 --> 00:08:12.580
and then you divide it
by the number of people.

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:13.770
That's per capita.

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:15.650
And you say, essentially,

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:19.150
if we divided our GDP perfectly evenly

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:21.030
which again, I don't recommend,

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:23.840
that's what would be happening.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.490
And so you can see it's really rising

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:28.530
quite nicely in some ways, right?

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:32.240
I mean, it's nearly an increase of 100%.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:33.440
So I'm sure most of the listeners

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:35.170
know would be a doubling, okay?

00:08:35.170 --> 00:08:37.620
So it hasn't quite doubled since 1980.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:39.170
This is adjusted for inflation,

00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:42.170
but it's come pretty close to doubling.

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:44.450
But then, you kind of get under that,

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:47.110
and you look at what's happened
to each economic group.

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.240
And you look at the bottom 50%.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.810
Well, their incomes have risen.

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:54.800
They're making more money
than they were in 1980,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.563
but they haven't risen that much.

00:08:57.563 --> 00:08:58.396
- [Sal] And to be clear,

00:08:58.396 --> 00:08:59.740
this is all an inflation adjusted.

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:00.847
So it's not just, you know, people say,

00:09:00.847 --> 00:09:02.190
"Oh, things were cheaper in 1950,"

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:04.930
but this is adjusted
arguably for inflation.

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:06.930
This is real income.

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:08.440
- This is adjusted for inflation.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:10.410
And there are fights about
exactly about how to do it,

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:11.780
and is it perfectly done,

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:14.000
or is it overstated or understated.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:15.840
And those are good, interesting fights.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:19.140
But the bottom line is if
you adjust for inflation,

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:20.400
what you're really trying capture

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.400
is the experience, people's lives.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.560
The fact that today, something, you know,

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:25.690
what's a Big Mac today?

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:26.523
Five bucks?

00:09:26.523 --> 00:09:29.870
The fact that it might have
cost two bucks in the past,

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.020
I hope I'm not wrong about that.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.230
I don't eat a lot of Big Macs,

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:36.770
that doesn't actually show
any significant difference

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:37.990
between it over time.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:39.960
So you adjust for that.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:43.040
So what you see here is
that for the bottom 50%

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.820
or the middle 40%, things have risen.

00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.420
You know when people say
that the middle class

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.270
today is making less money
than it did in the past,

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.450
I think that's wrong if you're looking

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:54.720
at the long-term past.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:56.950
But the increases have been modest.

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:58.800
And here, I think, is an important thing.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.630
They have been much
smaller than the increases

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:04.500
you would expect based
from what our economy

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:06.010
has been producing.

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:08.420
You then move up to what for shorthand,

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:09.660
I call the upper middle class,

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.450
the 90th to 99th percentile.

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:14.450
You've got a lot of doctors in there.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:15.570
You've got lawyers.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.420
You've got university professors.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:21.110
You've gotta a lot of
professionals, right?

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:25.020
Their income has tracked
actually quite closely

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:26.780
with per capita GDP.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:28.860
Not everyone's, but as a group.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:31.664
And then when you get to the top 1%,

00:10:31.664 --> 00:10:34.500
and really to the top of the top 1%,

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.710
the top .01%, you see

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:40.610
that they're on a completely
different trajectory.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:43.913
Their incomes are
tripling, are quintupling.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:49.400
And so when you see this, I
think it helps you realize

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:52.710
some of the frustrations that people have.

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:53.957
People feel like, "Wait a second,

00:10:53.957 --> 00:10:57.867
"even if I make more money
than my grandparents did,

00:10:57.867 --> 00:10:58.980
"than my parents did,"

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:01.690
which some people do
and some people don't,

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:05.430
it sure doesn't feel like
I'm keeping up with society

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:06.263
as a whole.

00:11:06.263 --> 00:11:07.210
And they're right about that.

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:09.410
And it's not just absolute changes

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:11.200
that people judge their lives on.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.290
There's a rich psychological
research that shows

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:14.760
it's relative changes as well.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:15.670
I mean, put it this way, Sal,

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:18.920
if you and I worked at a
company where profits doubled,

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:21.590
and you and I got a 10%
raise the previous year,

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:24.490
and we could look and see
a bunch of other workers

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:26.330
who got their pay tripled,

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:28.157
would we say, "Oh my
goodness, we're so happy

00:11:28.157 --> 00:11:29.720
"we got a 10% raise"?

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:30.977
Or would we say, "Wait a second,

00:11:30.977 --> 00:11:32.750
"this doesn't feel totally fair."

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:34.880
And I think that's how
a lot of Americans feel,

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:36.990
and they're right to feel that way.

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:39.200
- And before we get into potential causes

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:40.380
of these trends, I will ask,

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:42.100
and I'll play devil's
advocate a little bit

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:44.050
which is you know, there's this argument,

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:48.360
look, we have market forces, capitalism.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:50.860
As you mentioned, you don't
want absolutely equal.

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:53.960
You want people to wanna
invest and take risk,

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:55.110
and that might lead to some,

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:56.820
and there's bad and good luck

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:59.490
that leads to some level of inequality.

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:02.127
Some would argue that, "Hey, look.

00:12:02.127 --> 00:12:04.657
"At the end of the day, it
looks like everyone has improved

00:12:04.657 --> 00:12:06.027
"in real terms.

00:12:06.027 --> 00:12:08.310
"Why should we be so caught
up with the relative?"

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:09.143
Wouldn't you rather

00:12:09.143 --> 00:12:11.009
have a world where everyone is improving,

00:12:11.009 --> 00:12:13.060
maybe some more than others,

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:14.850
than a world where it's equal,

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:16.703
but we're not improving as much?

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:18.503
- Well, sure.

00:12:18.503 --> 00:12:20.600
And I think part of the
answer to that question

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:23.930
is this is definitely better
than some alternatives

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:25.070
you could imagine.

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:27.440
It's better than a world in which GDP

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.030
wasn't rising at all.

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:32.040
It's better than a world in
which only a small percentage

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:33.270
of people are seeing an increase

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:36.570
and no one else is seeing
any kind of increase.

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:40.180
But when you look across
the relative comparisons,

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:43.810
it seems to me it really
requires a stretch to argue

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:45.970
that this is the natural outcome.

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:47.440
So one relative comparison

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:49.380
would be other high-income countries

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:50.820
over the same period.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:53.000
They've also seen inequality rise,

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.340
but they haven't seen it rise

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:56.430
as much as the United States has.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:58.930
So some other countries
have figured out ways

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:01.560
to have middle class pay rise more rapidly

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.600
and pay for low income workers

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:07.530
rise more rapidly than our pay has risen.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:08.890
Maybe even more starkly,

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:11.080
those countries have also have had health

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:14.600
and life expectancy
grow substantially more

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:15.970
than the United States has.

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:18.850
So it seems like they're doing
something differently from us

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:23.010
and for much of the population,
most of the population,

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:24.520
it's working better.

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:28.063
Then you look at our own
country between the 1940s

00:13:28.063 --> 00:13:30.000
and the 1970s.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.970
By no means a perfect period,

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:34.490
a period in which opportunities for women

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:36.490
were highly constrained,

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:40.660
in which there was vicious,
often legally sanctioned racism

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:43.193
in which large numbers of
Americans couldn't vote.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:47.000
Again, by no means a perfect period.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.920
But when you look at these
same measures what you see

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:51.830
is that the pay of nearly every group,

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:54.160
if we look at this from
the '40s to the '80s,

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:56.340
'40s to the '70s, the pay of every group

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:58.660
would be much closer to that black line.

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:00.580
And not only that, but
the pay of the middle

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:02.570
would actually have
increased a little bit faster

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:03.970
than the pay at the top.

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:07.860
And so I think the burden,

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.320
the people who say this
is the natural outcome,

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:12.780
I think the burden is on them to explain,

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.340
well wait a second, why
is this a natural outcome

00:14:15.340 --> 00:14:17.860
if it's not what the
United States used to have,

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:21.010
and it's not what many
other countries have had?

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:24.540
- And so given that, let's
go into possible reasons

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:26.660
for why this either is
happening around the globe

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:28.470
which it sounds like it has
been happening to some degree,

00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:30.960
and in especially in the
case of the United States.

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:34.563
What do you think are the culprits here?

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.060
- So I think there are a few.

00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:38.970
And I think they're all fascinating.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:41.360
So I apologize, I'll be going through them

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:42.820
extremely quickly if you wanna dive

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:44.230
more into any one of them.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:45.770
I think it's clear that globalization

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:47.160
is part of the cause here.

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:49.980
And there's a lot that's
wonderful about globalization.

00:14:49.980 --> 00:14:52.500
I mean, for much of the late 20th century,

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:53.840
you had large parts of the world

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:56.230
that essentially weren't
competing in the global economy.

00:14:56.230 --> 00:14:58.110
Either they were too poor,

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:02.250
or they were part of the Soviet Empire

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:05.303
and essentially weren't able
to compete in the economy.

00:15:05.303 --> 00:15:07.960
Or they were Europe or Japan

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:10.010
which was essentially
destroyed by World War II,

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:10.843
both of which.

00:15:10.843 --> 00:15:14.550
So some of it is this entrance of billions

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:16.220
of people into the global economy

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:17.800
which has increased competition,

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.574
particularly for certain kinds of labor,

00:15:20.574 --> 00:15:22.920
and has created problems for people

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:24.483
in the more affluent world.

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:27.940
That's also wrapped up
with technological change

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:29.850
which plays a role there.

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:31.770
Then though, I think
there are a set of forces

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:33.420
we have essentially chosen.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:36.276
- And just to make sure we all make sure

00:15:36.276 --> 00:15:38.130
we understand those points

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:39.340
about globalization and technology

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:40.490
'cause I think they're similar.

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:42.106
You know, some would say,

00:15:42.106 --> 00:15:43.720
okay there's competition around the world,

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:45.830
but why is that
disproportionately affecting

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:46.840
the lower incomes?

00:15:46.840 --> 00:15:50.230
And your argument would be
technology and globalization.

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:52.850
You know, technology obviously
is now arguably a robot

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:56.040
or a computer might be able
to do parts of that job.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:58.840
So it kind of lowers the importance

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:01.430
maybe of labor, or at least
the negotiating capability.

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:03.530
And obviously if there's
someone in another country

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:05.360
who could do that same job

00:16:05.360 --> 00:16:08.900
and because trade barriers have gone down,

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:11.100
and monetary barriers have gone down,

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:13.240
people can relocate factories.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:14.100
And so now all of sudden,

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:17.510
that could put pressure on wages in the US

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:19.110
or in richer countries

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:22.490
if someone in another country
can do it for cheaper.

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:23.323
- That's precisely right.

00:16:23.323 --> 00:16:26.025
I mean, you take furniture
manufacturer in North Carolina

00:16:26.025 --> 00:16:29.020
has managed to move to Latin America

00:16:29.020 --> 00:16:33.510
or to Asia where workers to
do some of the same work,

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:34.530
or maybe not as efficiently.

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:36.510
But if they're being paid 1/5 as much,

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.750
it doesn't matter if they're
not as quite as efficient

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:39.583
in doing it.

00:16:39.583 --> 00:16:40.663
We've seen this again and again.

00:16:40.663 --> 00:16:44.200
If workers are being
replaced by farm workers,

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:46.190
they might be replaced by a machine

00:16:46.190 --> 00:16:48.380
that can do what they would do.

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:50.750
And so maybe you used to have 10 workers

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:55.430
doing something manually or
with a factory assembly line.

00:16:55.430 --> 00:16:59.430
Now you have two or three
workers operating computers

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:00.670
that make the same goods.

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:03.450
So those are true forces,

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:04.283
really important forces.
- And then just to be clear

00:17:04.283 --> 00:17:07.190
on globalization, the academic
argument for globalization

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:09.360
is it actually makes the pie larger.

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:12.500
And so the consumers of those
services or those goods,

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.170
they might be able to
get it for cheaper now.

00:17:14.170 --> 00:17:17.710
And also, what's probably
happening to your point

00:17:17.710 --> 00:17:20.500
is those who own the
technology or have the capital,

00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:22.200
they might be getting better returns now

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:23.670
because some of their
costs have gone down.

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:24.810
They're able to lower the price

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:26.770
so the consumers benefit,
but then they're able

00:17:26.770 --> 00:17:29.160
to get better profit because their costs

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:30.530
have gone down even more.

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:33.430
- Yeah, and then I would
argue maybe even bigger

00:17:33.430 --> 00:17:36.280
than either of those is
that it can bring good jobs

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:37.720
to people around the world.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:40.090
You know, the last 50 years
have seen the greatest reduction

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:42.320
in global poverty in recorded history.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:43.330
It hasn't been in this country.

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:44.416
It hasn't been in Europe,

00:17:44.416 --> 00:17:46.150
it hasn't been in Japan,

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:48.030
but it has been in China, in India,

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:50.243
in large parts of Africa
and South America.

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:54.764
And so that is a very good development

00:17:54.764 --> 00:17:56.990
for people who live there.

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:59.084
It's a good development for the world.

00:17:59.084 --> 00:18:00.017
- And that's a good disclaimer.

00:18:00.017 --> 00:18:01.930
And I can't wait to get
to all of the questions

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:02.763
we're getting, but I do,

00:18:02.763 --> 00:18:04.000
I wanna go through all of the levers.

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:06.630
But this is, you know, as
we talk about these levers,

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:08.500
even though a lever may be contributing

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:09.580
in some way to inequality,

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:11.070
we're not saying that it's bad.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:12.930
There might be other
positive aspects about it.

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:14.670
There might be ways to
mitigate those things.

00:18:14.670 --> 00:18:16.237
So I think very quickly,
you know, people say,

00:18:16.237 --> 00:18:17.177
"Wait, I like globalization.

00:18:17.177 --> 00:18:18.110
"I don't like globalization."

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:19.710
But it's like, no, well
there's just some things

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:20.830
that it might be contributing.

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:22.540
Might have benefits, might have negatives.

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:25.210
Same thing about technology.

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:26.430
So let's keep going through these levers,

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:29.130
then I'll open it up to some
of these questions I'm seeing.

00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:30.280
- That's great, I'm excited to hear them.

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:32.220
So I think then there are a set of things

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:34.860
where I would argue that
they are in many ways

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:38.020
policy choices that we have
made with either knowing

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:40.180
that we're making them, or
not necessarily knowing.

00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:44.180
So one of the really key changes

00:18:44.180 --> 00:18:46.520
is that we are no longer

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:52.570
producing educated, increasing
the stock of educated people

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:54.100
at the rate that we used to be.

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:56.400
There's a fantastic book by Claudia Goldin

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:57.637
and Larry Katz called

00:18:57.637 --> 00:19:00.030
"The Race Between
Education and Technology".

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:02.390
And the title captures a lot this,

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:04.240
which is the example I just gave

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:06.970
where you went from 10
workers to two workers.

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:08.900
Those two workers, need
to have higher skills

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:10.180
in order to operate computers.

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:12.100
And if you think of the
long sweep of history,

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:13.560
you can kind of imagine this race

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:15.260
between education and technology.

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:17.040
The technology gets more sophisticated.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:18.900
Are we producing the kind of workers

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:21.000
who can use the new technologies?

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:23.630
And there's been a huge slow
down in educational attainment

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:24.510
in this country,

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:28.470
even though all the signs
are that education pays off.

00:19:28.470 --> 00:19:31.670
And I know people say,
"Wait, is college overrated?"

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:32.520
But as I like to point out

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:33.920
the people who say, "Is
college overrated?",

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:35.670
are often making sure that their own kids

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:36.870
are going to college.

00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:38.690
So I think in terms of their own behavior,

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:41.380
people understand that
education is not overrated.

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:43.440
The unemployment rate
for college graduates

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:46.580
is vastly lower than for
non-college graduates.

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:49.340
The pay gap is near an all-time record.

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:51.530
College graduates live longer.

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:53.180
They are more likely to be happy.

00:19:53.180 --> 00:19:55.000
They're more likely to be healthy.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:57.570
And so but we haven't
done a good enough job

00:19:57.570 --> 00:19:59.510
allowing more people to go to college

00:19:59.510 --> 00:20:00.750
and really, to complete college.

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:02.367
So education is one issue.

00:20:02.367 --> 00:20:04.132
- And actually, I'll double back

00:20:04.132 --> 00:20:05.023
'cause there's a good question on that.

00:20:05.023 --> 00:20:06.769
There's so many.

00:20:06.769 --> 00:20:08.300
I hope we have a little bit more time

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:10.260
because I wanna get through all of these.

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:11.950
But there is a question that relates

00:20:11.950 --> 00:20:13.530
to your point about the college education

00:20:13.530 --> 00:20:14.460
which I think is interesting.

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.307
Mamata Barachariah says,

00:20:17.307 --> 00:20:21.677
"I often think we can
get rid of inequality

00:20:21.677 --> 00:20:22.920
"by educating everyone who wants

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:24.577
to be educated to your point,

00:20:24.577 --> 00:20:26.427
"but don't you think this
crazy college tuition

00:20:26.427 --> 00:20:30.040
"is one of the reasons behind
the massive inequality?"

00:20:30.040 --> 00:20:33.180
And I also have heard the data

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:35.510
of those with a college education

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:37.740
make X amount more and
et cetera, et cetera.

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:40.350
But you know, it's not
a causality necessarily.

00:20:40.350 --> 00:20:42.120
It could be a correlation.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:45.050
And so we're getting a lot more people

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:45.883
to go into it.

00:20:45.883 --> 00:20:47.900
They're getting into
large amounts of debt,

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:50.690
debt that's equivalent to the down payment

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:52.830
for their house in New York or California,

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:54.360
and probably buying a house outright

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:55.633
in a lot of the country.

00:20:56.790 --> 00:20:59.260
Do you think, and maybe
that's not even captured

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:01.530
into some of these inflation numbers.

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:03.093
What's your sense of that?

00:21:03.930 --> 00:21:05.960
- So I think it's
important to separate this.

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:08.900
The cost of college is a real problem,

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:12.220
but it's often, I think,
mis-described a little bit.

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:13.870
People hear, you know, it costs,

00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:16.220
you're out in California,
I'm out in Washington.

00:21:16.220 --> 00:21:17.990
You know, it costs, what, $70,000 a year

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:19.680
to go to Stanford, or to go to Georgetown

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:22.100
or to go to a school like that.

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:23.480
But it's really important to remember

00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:25.200
those are not normal schools.

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:26.660
And not only that, but the schools

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:28.930
that charge that tend
to give large amounts

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:32.030
of financial aid to middle
and lower income people.

00:21:32.030 --> 00:21:33.600
The typical college,

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:35.810
you know, like a California
State University,

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:38.660
or the University of
Maryland at Baltimore County,

00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:39.800
or you name it,

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:42.370
these places cost vastly, vastly less.

00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:43.800
They still cost a lot.

00:21:43.800 --> 00:21:46.210
And I think they probably cost too much,

00:21:46.210 --> 00:21:48.920
but you can't end up going
to one of those places

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:52.660
and end up with $200,000 worth of debt.

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:53.670
You know, the average debt

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:56.110
for college graduates is around $30,000.

00:21:56.110 --> 00:21:57.414
That's not nothing.

00:21:57.414 --> 00:22:00.060
But it's much, much less than the gap.

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:02.360
So cost is a problem.

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:04.760
But still, the truth is that
the vast majority of people

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:08.670
who finish easily pay off their debt.

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:10.663
Not everybody, but the vast majority.

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:14.420
College debt is a much
bigger problem for dropouts.

00:22:14.420 --> 00:22:16.180
College debt is a bigger problem

00:22:16.180 --> 00:22:18.570
for people who end up
with $15,000 of debt,

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:20.250
but don't get a degree.

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:23.073
We don't talk about that
crisis nearly often enough.

00:22:24.783 --> 00:22:26.860
And then the only other thing I'd say

00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:29.260
is there'd been some really
good academic studies

00:22:29.260 --> 00:22:31.410
that have studied people
right at the threshold

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:35.820
of getting into the least
selective four-year college

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:37.193
in a state like Florida.

00:22:38.060 --> 00:22:40.660
And the people who were
just above that threshold,

00:22:40.660 --> 00:22:43.780
and who just get into the least
selective four-year college

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:46.320
and thus have a shot to
go to a four-year school

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:48.210
actually end up doing substantially better

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:49.570
in life over their 20s,

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:51.033
that's just as far as the data's gone out,

00:22:51.033 --> 00:22:53.410
than people who just
missed that threshold.

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:54.970
And so while clearly part

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:57.110
of the value of college is correlation,

00:22:57.110 --> 00:22:59.253
I do think part of it is also causality.

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:02.077
- Yeah, no, that last study
sounds really interesting.

00:23:02.077 --> 00:23:04.770
And look, I can personally testify.

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:06.180
And I went to one of those schools

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:07.640
that I didn't think I could afford

00:23:07.640 --> 00:23:10.110
'cause the MSRP price was very high.

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:12.160
But I was able to go even though my mother

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:15.330
made $16,000 a year in 1993.

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:18.010
So I can completely vouch for that.

00:23:18.010 --> 00:23:20.360
But anyway, I interrupted
the various levers.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:24.503
So we talked about globalization,
technology, and education.

00:23:24.503 --> 00:23:26.010
Let's keep going.

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:26.843
- Yeah, yeah.

00:23:26.843 --> 00:23:29.620
So I think you can combine
immigration and trade policy.

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:32.160
I think sometimes people
overstate the importance

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:32.993
of trade policy.

00:23:32.993 --> 00:23:34.960
It's not like the trade deals we've signed

00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:36.890
have caused all these problems.

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:38.810
But some of them really
have contributed to it.

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:41.540
And basically what we've
done is we have allowed

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:43.800
our both immigration and our trade policy

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:46.580
to create much more
competition in the middle

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:48.470
and the bottom of the labor market

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:49.303
than at the top.

00:23:49.303 --> 00:23:52.810
So as you probably know,
it's like impossible

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:54.760
if you're a doctor from another country

00:23:54.760 --> 00:23:56.190
to move to this country

00:23:56.190 --> 00:23:57.570
and become a doctor, right?

00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.750
You kind of have to start
all over at the beginning.

00:23:59.750 --> 00:24:02.580
That's basically a big
trade barrier that protects

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:05.030
doctors in this country, right?

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:06.600
We say it's because we don't trust

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:07.960
those other medical systems,

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:09.300
but there are plenty of easy ways

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:10.360
to figure out whether doctors

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:12.990
are actually qualified
without simply rejecting

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:15.650
all of these qualifications
from other countries.

00:24:15.650 --> 00:24:16.780
But we don't do that.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:20.187
We make it really hard to
become a doctor in this country.

00:24:20.187 --> 00:24:23.840
On the other hand, we've
had this absolute surge

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:26.550
of lower skilled and
medium skilled immigration

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:28.530
into this country over the last 30 years.

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:30.240
That's had some big benefits,

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:33.300
but it also really has created
additional competition.

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:34.780
And so when you look at our trade

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:36.300
and our immigration policies,

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:39.160
many of them really make sense

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:41.030
that they have put more
pressure on the wages

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:42.450
of people in the middle and the bottom

00:24:42.450 --> 00:24:43.283
than at the top.

00:24:43.283 --> 00:24:45.180
And then I would just
very briefly after that,

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:46.520
our tax policy.

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:49.250
Taxes on the very rich have plummeted.

00:24:49.250 --> 00:24:51.970
The estate tax, income
taxes, corporate taxes,

00:24:51.970 --> 00:24:54.130
every tax that the very rich pay

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:56.750
have basically plummeted
over the last 40 years.

00:24:56.750 --> 00:24:59.040
And taxes for the people in the middle

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:01.140
and at the bottom which very important,

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:03.970
there tend to be the
payroll tax and sales taxes,

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:05.910
have just not fallen very much.

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:08.800
And so over the same
period that the market

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:10.760
has caused inequality to rise,

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:14.870
our tax code has exacerbated
those changes rather than

00:25:14.870 --> 00:25:16.120
pushing against it.
- Philippe,

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:18.010
if you could bring up that animation

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:20.880
from David's Twitter account on how tax

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:21.860
has changed over time.

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:23.760
I think that really hits the point home

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:25.360
of what David's just mentioning.

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:28.690
There's an animation
that I really enjoyed,

00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:29.920
and I actually made a video with it.

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:30.753
Yeah, here we go.

00:25:30.753 --> 00:25:31.840
And what you see,

00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:33.400
actually, you could explain this.

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:34.233
What's going?

00:25:34.233 --> 00:25:35.813
It keeps repeating itself.

00:25:35.813 --> 00:25:37.320
- [David] So this is the total tax rate.

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:39.790
You combine federal state and local

00:25:39.790 --> 00:25:42.820
for the very richest families.

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:43.653
I can't remember.

00:25:43.653 --> 00:25:46.280
You may see it on there as the top .01%.

00:25:46.280 --> 00:25:48.354
You know, it's something
at the very, very top.

00:25:48.354 --> 00:25:51.970
- [Sal] And what you're
saying that the right side,

00:25:51.970 --> 00:25:54.070
the right data point is for the richest?

00:25:54.070 --> 00:25:54.903
- Yes, thank you, sorry.

00:25:54.903 --> 00:25:55.760
This is the whole tax.

00:25:55.760 --> 00:25:57.032
Exactly right, Sal.

00:25:57.032 --> 00:25:58.740
This is the whole tax.

00:25:58.740 --> 00:26:00.012
This is the country, right?

00:26:00.012 --> 00:26:01.600
And so what you can see is in the middle

00:26:01.600 --> 00:26:04.170
and the bottom, these tax rates
aren't changing very much.

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:07.290
They're staying within
a fairly narrow range.

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:08.720
That's the yellow line and the middle

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:09.830
and to the left.

00:26:09.830 --> 00:26:11.610
But to the very right, where you're seeing

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:13.340
the very, very richest folks,

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:15.160
the tax rate is plummeting over time.

00:26:15.160 --> 00:26:16.300
And I wanna emphasize,

00:26:16.300 --> 00:26:17.930
this is actual tax rate.

00:26:17.930 --> 00:26:19.700
This isn't the statutory tax rate

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:21.110
you pay on the last dollar.

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:23.310
This is you take someone's total income.

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:25.640
You then take all the taxes they pay,

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:26.830
federal, state, and local.

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:29.460
And back in the 1950s,
the very, very wealthy

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:33.820
were paying more than half
of all their income in taxes.

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:34.980
Maybe that's too high.

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:36.380
May well have been too high.

00:26:37.626 --> 00:26:40.380
But it's plummeted to the
point where out tax code now

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:42.063
looks extremely flat.

00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:45.390
There are fights among economists about

00:26:45.390 --> 00:26:46.560
is it totally flat?

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:48.540
Do the rich still pay somewhat more?

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:50.580
But the data all points
in the same direction

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:53.000
which is it's become much much flatter

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:54.470
than it used to be over time

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:56.760
because we've cut taxes on stocks,

00:26:56.760 --> 00:26:59.080
on other investments,

00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:03.181
on inheritances, and on very top incomes.

00:27:03.181 --> 00:27:05.350
And we haven't done that to sales taxes

00:27:05.350 --> 00:27:07.160
and payroll taxes.

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:09.520
- [David] And just to make
sure people understand,

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:11.360
each, if you know at the horizontal axis,

00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:13.920
the left is the lowest income earners.

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:16.500
And then you go to the 30%
percentile, 50th, 70th,

00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:20.270
90th, 99th, and then 99.99th,

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:22.430
and then the top 400 people.

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:24.520
And then the vertical
axis is the tax rate,

00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:25.850
effective tax rate to your point.

00:27:25.850 --> 00:27:27.960
It's the total amount they paid,

00:27:27.960 --> 00:27:30.930
I guess, divided by their income.

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:33.320
And to your point, you see how this flows

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:37.260
from 1950 all the way to
close to present times.

00:27:37.260 --> 00:27:42.260
And yeah, the zero to the
90th percentile or so,

00:27:42.470 --> 00:27:45.540
it's been pretty much the
same tax rate, give or take.

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:48.250
While it's really that
top 400 or the 99.99th,

00:27:48.250 --> 00:27:50.760
or even the 99th percentile
that's come down a good bit.

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:52.550
And you know where it ends is fascinating

00:27:52.550 --> 00:27:54.900
because it ends in 2018,

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:58.960
where the total effective
tax rates for the top 400

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:01.920
is lower than the 99.99th

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:04.460
which is lower than the
group right before it.

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:05.770
So how does that happen?

00:28:05.770 --> 00:28:07.807
'Cause you know, when I
fill out my taxes I see

00:28:07.807 --> 00:28:10.010
for every incremental more you make,

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:11.650
they're taxing at a higher amount.

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:15.940
So how is it possible that
the top 400 are taxed lower

00:28:15.940 --> 00:28:18.740
than the 99.99th percentile?

00:28:18.740 --> 00:28:20.770
- You may remember a number of years ago,

00:28:20.770 --> 00:28:22.500
Warren Buffett got a bunch of attention

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:24.220
by saying that he paid a lower tax rate

00:28:24.220 --> 00:28:25.750
than his secretary.

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:27.710
And Warren Buffett is very good

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:30.091
at explaining things pitifully.

00:28:30.091 --> 00:28:32.730
And in some ways, that's
what he's describing

00:28:32.730 --> 00:28:34.000
or what you see here.

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:37.550
One of the reasons is
that we tax investments

00:28:37.550 --> 00:28:38.403
at a lower rate.

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:42.270
The people setting policy have decided

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:44.560
that if you tax investments
at a lower rate,

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:46.420
you'll get much more investment.

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:48.360
That'll benefit everyone long-term.

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:50.280
It hasn't quite worked out
the way it's been sold,

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:51.570
but that's the theory.

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:55.500
And so basically we tax
things like stock returns

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:58.300
at a lower rate we tax labor income.

00:28:58.300 --> 00:29:01.700
And the people on the
far right of this chart

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:06.230
have much more of their
income in capital investments,

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:11.230
in stocks than they do relative
to the people in the middle.

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:12.140
- Yeah, and actually, I asked

00:29:12.140 --> 00:29:13.380
that last question somewhat naively.

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:15.730
My last career, I was
a hedge fund analyst.

00:29:15.730 --> 00:29:17.730
And I remember, I mean, my boss

00:29:17.730 --> 00:29:20.510
who was a portfolio manager
and was the partner,

00:29:20.510 --> 00:29:21.750
he felt guilty about it.

00:29:21.750 --> 00:29:22.583
He, a great guy.

00:29:22.583 --> 00:29:23.440
He actually would donate.

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:25.910
We once made a good
investment on an egg company,

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:28.170
and he felt so bad
because he's a vegetarian

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:30.350
that he donated all of his profits

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:31.686
for humane treatment of chickens.

00:29:31.686 --> 00:29:35.050
But he felt guilty when
I learned about this.

00:29:35.050 --> 00:29:37.720
I was paying a higher
tax rate as an analyst

00:29:37.720 --> 00:29:39.280
because that was just income

00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:40.380
versus what he was paying

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:44.300
because his income was classified
as capital gains income.

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:45.900
So he was paying roughly 15%

00:29:45.900 --> 00:29:49.500
while I was paying 40 or 50%.

00:29:49.500 --> 00:29:54.030
So it is a fascinating phenomena.

00:29:54.030 --> 00:29:55.190
So we have a ton of questions here.

00:29:55.190 --> 00:29:57.470
We're already at our normal end time,

00:29:57.470 --> 00:29:58.760
but I wanna make sure we
get to some questions.

00:29:58.760 --> 00:30:00.050
Let me know, David, if you've gotta run

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:01.930
or if you're running into another session.

00:30:01.930 --> 00:30:04.560
But you know, one question
that's been coming up a lot,

00:30:04.560 --> 00:30:09.077
even before COVID, you know,
Maya Sharma from YouTube asks,

00:30:09.077 --> 00:30:10.827
"Do you think universal basic income

00:30:10.827 --> 00:30:13.307
"is a valid solution in the long-term?"

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:19.220
- So I mean, look, this is a
great matter for debate, right?

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:22.290
And if we have any high school
debaters listening today,

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:24.230
I know that universal basic income

00:30:24.230 --> 00:30:25.700
was one of the big topics

00:30:25.700 --> 00:30:28.680
that was debated on the high
school circuit last year.

00:30:28.680 --> 00:30:31.950
And it seems like it's actually
led to a lot of debates

00:30:31.950 --> 00:30:32.950
that people really liked

00:30:32.950 --> 00:30:36.220
because there are very good
philosophical arguments

00:30:36.220 --> 00:30:37.380
on both sides of it.

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:41.160
What I would say is I
think that it really does

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:44.320
have some of the same incentive problems

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:46.360
that we talked about
with some of the issues

00:30:46.360 --> 00:30:48.940
of societies that tried
to get rid of inequality.

00:30:48.940 --> 00:30:50.830
I know universal basic income

00:30:50.830 --> 00:30:53.040
isn't trying to get rid of inequality,

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:56.140
but I think when you look at
what really, really matters

00:30:56.140 --> 00:30:59.840
to people, what correlates
with good health,

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:01.670
what correlates with happiness,

00:31:01.670 --> 00:31:03.600
it often does involve work.

00:31:03.600 --> 00:31:06.610
It involves the dignity that work brings.

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:09.478
It involves feeling like you're
part of something larger,

00:31:09.478 --> 00:31:11.530
a larger project.

00:31:11.530 --> 00:31:14.260
And so I have some real skepticism

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:16.100
about universal basic income

00:31:16.100 --> 00:31:17.651
as our solution.

00:31:17.651 --> 00:31:20.840
And I would be much more interested

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:25.160
in a set of solutions
where we really tried

00:31:25.160 --> 00:31:28.190
to substantially increase the returns

00:31:28.190 --> 00:31:29.550
to what are now low wage work.

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:31.680
You can do that through a
much higher minimum wage.

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:32.880
You can do it through a much bigger

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:34.379
earned income tax credit.

00:31:34.379 --> 00:31:37.170
And then, maybe also looked at the idea

00:31:37.170 --> 00:31:39.750
of rewarding people more for what are now

00:31:39.750 --> 00:31:43.550
unpaid forms of labor, like
child care and elder care.

00:31:43.550 --> 00:31:46.280
But to me, the idea of
a universal basic income

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:48.500
where we're paying people not to work,

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:51.830
despite everything we know
about some of the health

00:31:51.830 --> 00:31:56.070
and psychological costs to not working,

00:31:56.070 --> 00:32:00.220
and in addition, Sal, I
don't think I need to receive

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:01.530
universal basic income.

00:32:01.530 --> 00:32:03.780
You know, I have a job as a journalist

00:32:03.780 --> 00:32:05.430
and it pays me enough to live on.

00:32:05.430 --> 00:32:06.810
And I really don't think our government

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:09.870
should be sending me a check every month.

00:32:09.870 --> 00:32:11.870
And you know, there are millions of people

00:32:11.870 --> 00:32:13.810
who also don't need to be
getting a check every month.

00:32:13.810 --> 00:32:16.030
So I'm a skeptic of
universal basic income,

00:32:16.030 --> 00:32:17.780
but I also see the arguments for it,

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:20.890
and I think it's a good thing

00:32:20.890 --> 00:32:23.623
that we as a society have
started to debate it more.

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:26.290
- Yeah, and I think the argument would be,

00:32:26.290 --> 00:32:27.310
if we received checks,

00:32:27.310 --> 00:32:29.000
we'll also on the other
side be taxed higher,

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:30.550
so it would kind of be a wash.

00:32:30.550 --> 00:32:32.670
But it's easier to implement things

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:34.860
like other social safety nets

00:32:34.860 --> 00:32:37.160
created some of that disincentive
that you talked about.

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:39.050
While universal basic income,
you could still have a job.

00:32:39.050 --> 00:32:40.660
But yeah, it's an interesting debate.

00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:41.650
You know, one of the things you brought up

00:32:41.650 --> 00:32:42.683
was minimum wage.

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:47.547
An economic purist would have
an argument against it saying,

00:32:47.547 --> 00:32:51.427
"Hey, if you artificially
raise the market-clearing wage,

00:32:51.427 --> 00:32:54.857
"then companies, your classic economics

00:32:54.857 --> 00:32:56.217
"with your supply and demand curve,

00:32:56.217 --> 00:32:58.337
"you're now going to create
this dead weight loss

00:32:58.337 --> 00:33:00.620
"where you're not going to hire everyone."

00:33:00.620 --> 00:33:03.100
There's also arguments that
it could create incentives

00:33:03.100 --> 00:33:07.540
to move even faster towards
technology or globalization.

00:33:07.540 --> 00:33:10.930
What are your counterarguments
to those arguments?

00:33:10.930 --> 00:33:12.500
- I mean, I think there's
both the philosophical

00:33:12.500 --> 00:33:14.600
and empirical counterargument.

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:17.120
There's been a real flowering of research

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:19.830
in the minimum wage over
the last 20 to 30 years.

00:33:19.830 --> 00:33:23.570
And some of it shows that
there is a trade-off,

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:25.230
that if you put in place a minimum wage,

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:26.930
you lose some jobs.

00:33:26.930 --> 00:33:29.270
The bulk of it shows
that the number of jobs

00:33:29.270 --> 00:33:32.160
you lose from increasing the
minimum wage, within reason.

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:34.260
If we went to a $50 an hour minimum wage,

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:35.170
who knows what would happen.

00:33:35.170 --> 00:33:36.768
We'd presumably lose a lot more jobs.

00:33:36.768 --> 00:33:40.580
The losses are actually much more modest.

00:33:40.580 --> 00:33:45.370
It's not the small, relatively
small dollar changes

00:33:45.370 --> 00:33:48.360
don't immediately lead to
declines in employment.

00:33:48.360 --> 00:33:49.530
There's a famous study looking

00:33:49.530 --> 00:33:51.380
at New Jersey and Pennsylvania,

00:33:51.380 --> 00:33:52.890
when one increased their minimum wage,

00:33:52.890 --> 00:33:53.723
and the other didn't.

00:33:53.723 --> 00:33:56.420
And so in terms of a
perfect economic laboratory,

00:33:56.420 --> 00:33:57.870
you'd imagine that a whole bunch of jobs

00:33:57.870 --> 00:33:59.970
on one side of the border
would move to the other,

00:33:59.970 --> 00:34:00.960
but they didn't.

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:03.650
And so it turns out that
people make decisions

00:34:03.650 --> 00:34:06.550
for all kinds of complicated reasons.

00:34:06.550 --> 00:34:09.030
And the minimum wage,
often, for a business

00:34:09.030 --> 00:34:11.980
doesn't lead to some radical
reduction in employment.

00:34:11.980 --> 00:34:14.410
And it can lead, quite quickly,

00:34:14.410 --> 00:34:16.640
to big changes in the quality of life

00:34:16.640 --> 00:34:20.070
of people who are
receiving a 20% increase,

00:34:20.070 --> 00:34:22.210
you know, $5 an hour to $6 an hour.

00:34:22.210 --> 00:34:24.750
That's a 20% increase in pay.

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:27.750
And so I think if you
kind of look empirically

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:30.670
at the research, it suggests
that minimum wage increases

00:34:30.670 --> 00:34:33.240
have done more good than
harm most of the time.

00:34:33.240 --> 00:34:34.530
And then I think philosophically,

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:36.717
I understand why a society might say,

00:34:36.717 --> 00:34:38.457
"Look, we're a wealthy society.

00:34:38.457 --> 00:34:40.737
"We're simply not willing to have

00:34:40.737 --> 00:34:44.477
"People living below a certain standard.

00:34:44.477 --> 00:34:46.677
"And so we're gonna set
a minimum standard."

00:34:48.880 --> 00:34:50.490
- Going back to either in minimum wage,

00:34:50.490 --> 00:34:52.260
or even this notion of
universal basic income,

00:34:52.260 --> 00:34:53.900
it does feel like the COVID crisis,

00:34:53.900 --> 00:34:56.470
obviously, for no fault of their own,

00:34:56.470 --> 00:34:58.970
many millions of people are
losing their jobs right now.

00:34:58.970 --> 00:35:02.490
Restaurants are either temporarily
or permanently shut down.

00:35:02.490 --> 00:35:04.990
Airlines, hotels, we could
make a long, long list.

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:08.080
To some degree, some of
what the stimulus packages

00:35:08.080 --> 00:35:10.220
have done is starting to look a little bit

00:35:10.220 --> 00:35:12.160
like universal basic income.

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:14.853
You know, every gets a $1,600 check.

00:35:16.469 --> 00:35:18.660
Unemployment insurance is extended,

00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:19.680
even if you're a contractor.

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:21.920
So do you think we're going
to naturally evolve there,

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:23.500
if this COVID crisis,

00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:25.875
we have 30 million unemployed,

00:35:25.875 --> 00:35:27.683
35 million unemployed.

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:29.750
People need to be fed.

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:32.043
People need to just be
able to pay their rent.

00:35:33.073 --> 00:35:35.580
- I would be surprised if this leads us

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:37.230
to universal basic income

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:39.850
because sending checks to people

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:41.930
is extremely expensive, right?

00:35:41.930 --> 00:35:43.560
The math, that's just the way the math is.

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:45.310
You're sending them to everyone

00:35:45.310 --> 00:35:46.683
and they're not that small.

00:35:47.780 --> 00:35:50.060
And look, we're still
gonna need to collect

00:35:50.060 --> 00:35:51.080
the data on this,

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:53.710
but my instinct is that
these checks being sent

00:35:53.710 --> 00:35:56.550
to everyone are not particularly

00:35:56.550 --> 00:35:58.670
efficient forms of stimulus.

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:00.830
They are not a very good
bang for the buck way

00:36:00.830 --> 00:36:02.420
to keep the economy functioning.

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:05.230
And I think there's a logical
way to think through this.

00:36:05.230 --> 00:36:07.360
So many, many people,

00:36:07.360 --> 00:36:10.570
most people have not lost their jobs.

00:36:10.570 --> 00:36:13.120
Not only have they not lost their jobs,

00:36:13.120 --> 00:36:15.280
many of them have had their typical daily

00:36:15.280 --> 00:36:17.820
or weekly expenses plummet, right?

00:36:17.820 --> 00:36:19.750
They're not paying for gas anymore.

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:21.240
They're not paying for the bus.

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:22.420
They're not paying for subways.

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:24.670
I don't know about you, but the amount

00:36:24.670 --> 00:36:26.610
of money I spend on food has plummeted

00:36:26.610 --> 00:36:29.750
because instead of picking up a $2 coffee

00:36:29.750 --> 00:36:33.860
or a $6 sandwich, I'm
making myself peanut butter

00:36:33.860 --> 00:36:34.810
on bread for lunch

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:36.510
'cause I'm around my refrigerator.

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:40.570
And so these people who
have kept their jobs

00:36:40.570 --> 00:36:42.630
and had their expenses go down,

00:36:42.630 --> 00:36:45.760
millions of them have
just gotten these checks.

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:47.490
And I think the evidence is showing

00:36:47.490 --> 00:36:49.380
that they're not actually
gonna spend that money.

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:50.550
They're gonna save it.

00:36:50.550 --> 00:36:53.923
Yet, now you take someone
who did lose her job.

00:36:54.890 --> 00:36:56.710
For that person, the amount of money

00:36:56.710 --> 00:36:59.430
that we are sending them
isn't nearly sufficient

00:36:59.430 --> 00:37:01.776
to cover the rent, to cover food,

00:37:01.776 --> 00:37:03.690
to pay for childcare.

00:37:03.690 --> 00:37:06.340
And so we've got this
slightly funny situation

00:37:06.340 --> 00:37:08.090
in which we've spent huge amounts of money

00:37:08.090 --> 00:37:11.610
spent sending $1,200 to everybody below

00:37:11.610 --> 00:37:13.270
a certain income threshold.

00:37:13.270 --> 00:37:15.690
For many people, that's
more than they need.

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:18.370
And for other people,
it's not nearly enough.

00:37:18.370 --> 00:37:19.690
And yet, it's so expensive

00:37:19.690 --> 00:37:22.020
that if we wanted to keep
doing it in perpetuity,

00:37:22.020 --> 00:37:25.300
we'd have to do massive cuts
to other social programs

00:37:25.300 --> 00:37:26.510
like Medicare and Medicaid.

00:37:26.510 --> 00:37:29.810
And so I'd be surprised if
it's where we end up long-term,

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:32.353
but lots of surprising things happen.

00:37:33.260 --> 00:37:36.050
- And what would you, you
know, if you were emperor,

00:37:36.050 --> 00:37:38.120
and you were going through
this COVID situation,

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:41.150
what would be your course of action?

00:37:41.150 --> 00:37:43.520
You know, I've heard,
we had Ray Dalio on here

00:37:43.520 --> 00:37:45.840
and I even chatted with him
a little bit afterwards.

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:48.920
And he's like by spending trillions

00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:50.520
and just pumping it into the economy.

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:52.770
Yeah, this is increasing
the debt by trillions,

00:37:52.770 --> 00:37:54.790
but the alternative is worse,

00:37:54.790 --> 00:37:57.460
that we go into a depression
if we don't do that.

00:37:57.460 --> 00:38:00.070
So would you be in favor
of that type of thing,

00:38:00.070 --> 00:38:01.930
and if so, what is the metric?

00:38:01.930 --> 00:38:03.730
Is it by writing these checks to everyone?

00:38:03.730 --> 00:38:05.260
Which it sounds like it wouldn't be.

00:38:05.260 --> 00:38:08.500
Is these loan programs which
seam to have been misused

00:38:08.500 --> 00:38:09.870
a little bit by certain corporations.

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:13.450
So how would you try to tackle this?

00:38:13.450 --> 00:38:15.250
- Austan Goolsbee, who's an economist

00:38:15.250 --> 00:38:17.130
to the University of Chicago,

00:38:17.130 --> 00:38:19.560
has this nice kind of series of lessons

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:21.500
that he put on Twitter, very quick,

00:38:21.500 --> 00:38:22.770
about virus economics.

00:38:22.770 --> 00:38:24.040
And I'm gonna misstate it slightly.

00:38:24.040 --> 00:38:25.290
I apologize to Austan.

00:38:25.290 --> 00:38:27.350
But the first rule of virus economics

00:38:27.350 --> 00:38:30.380
is the only way to help the
economy is to defeat the virus.

00:38:30.380 --> 00:38:31.920
And I think that's exactly right.

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:34.900
So I think we can't get back to normal

00:38:34.900 --> 00:38:37.800
if people are still
fearing for their lives,

00:38:37.800 --> 00:38:39.910
if they're fearing
particularly for their parents'

00:38:39.910 --> 00:38:41.970
and their grandparents' lives.

00:38:41.970 --> 00:38:43.200
It just won't happen.

00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:45.920
And so I think the most effective forms

00:38:45.920 --> 00:38:47.090
of economic stimulus

00:38:47.090 --> 00:38:50.240
are likely to be something
like a massive program

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:52.020
to increase testing capacity.

00:38:52.020 --> 00:38:54.350
All the estimates I've seen
that while the United States

00:38:54.350 --> 00:38:57.350
is doing more testing, we're
not doing nearly enough.

00:38:57.350 --> 00:38:59.450
A big program to increase quarantining,

00:38:59.450 --> 00:39:02.970
a big program to increase
protective equipment

00:39:02.970 --> 00:39:04.070
for medical workers,

00:39:04.070 --> 00:39:06.400
a big program to increase contact tracing.

00:39:06.400 --> 00:39:10.020
And of course, more research
into vaccines and treatments.

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:12.420
The good news is, while that
stuff's really expensive,

00:39:12.420 --> 00:39:13.950
it pales in comparison

00:39:13.950 --> 00:39:15.960
to the kind of trillions of dollars

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:18.010
that congress has spent to try to prop

00:39:18.010 --> 00:39:19.110
up the economy.

00:39:19.110 --> 00:39:21.740
So I think if you can
think of the economic stuff

00:39:21.740 --> 00:39:25.230
as being necessary, but
really just being a prop

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:27.830
that tries to get us through a period

00:39:27.830 --> 00:39:30.390
where we are investing enormous amounts

00:39:30.390 --> 00:39:32.620
in crushing the curve,

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:34.860
and then allowing us to begin to operate

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:36.630
and also get a vaccine.

00:39:36.630 --> 00:39:40.120
That strikes me as probably
the most effective approach.

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:42.140
But it doesn't really
seem to be the approach

00:39:42.140 --> 00:39:44.230
that we're following as a society.

00:39:44.230 --> 00:39:46.220
Instead, we've got this
scattershot approach

00:39:46.220 --> 00:39:47.960
in which we're shoveling massive amounts

00:39:47.960 --> 00:39:49.900
of money out the door which I do think

00:39:49.900 --> 00:39:51.070
is probably necessary,

00:39:51.070 --> 00:39:53.490
even if it's being done
in some inefficient ways.

00:39:53.490 --> 00:39:55.650
And we're not really committing ourselves

00:39:55.650 --> 00:39:57.690
to a huge national project

00:39:57.690 --> 00:39:59.130
to increase testing.

00:39:59.130 --> 00:40:01.530
And we're now starting
to open up even though

00:40:01.530 --> 00:40:03.120
we haven't hit many of the benchmarks

00:40:03.120 --> 00:40:05.750
that epidemiologists say we should hit.

00:40:05.750 --> 00:40:10.050
And so I'd love to be proven
too pessimistic about this,

00:40:10.050 --> 00:40:11.860
and there really is uncertainty.

00:40:11.860 --> 00:40:14.660
But I feel like the most likely scenario

00:40:14.660 --> 00:40:16.560
is that we're opening up too soon,

00:40:16.560 --> 00:40:17.950
and we are gonna pay a price

00:40:17.950 --> 00:40:20.980
both in terms of further outbreaks,

00:40:20.980 --> 00:40:25.000
but also in terms of
our economy sliding back

00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:27.570
into some kind of lockdown
and further recession

00:40:27.570 --> 00:40:29.483
when the outbreaks come.

00:40:30.340 --> 00:40:32.560
- Yeah, your point is no
matter how much stimulus you

00:40:32.560 --> 00:40:35.570
put in, if people are
still afraid to go outside,

00:40:35.570 --> 00:40:38.310
afraid to participate in the economy,

00:40:38.310 --> 00:40:39.910
the stimulus will kind
of be just like pushing

00:40:39.910 --> 00:40:42.437
on a string.

00:40:42.437 --> 00:40:45.610
And I'll add one area
for anybody listening,

00:40:45.610 --> 00:40:47.760
who's looking to spend a
few billion here or there.

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:50.280
I think internet access for everyone,

00:40:50.280 --> 00:40:53.120
especially in this time, is
a very, very good investment.

00:40:53.120 --> 00:40:55.100
I think it would pay many
good economic dividends.

00:40:55.100 --> 00:40:57.490
And if you look at it
compared to, you know,

00:40:57.490 --> 00:40:59.070
I've done back of the
envelope calculations.

00:40:59.070 --> 00:41:01.720
I think you could get all the families

00:41:01.720 --> 00:41:02.890
who don't have access today

00:41:02.890 --> 00:41:06.818
some kind of basic internet
that you can use Kahn Academy on

00:41:06.818 --> 00:41:08.980
for on the order of a
few tens of billions.

00:41:08.980 --> 00:41:10.700
Which sounds like a large number,

00:41:10.700 --> 00:41:14.840
but that's about 1% of the amount of money

00:41:14.840 --> 00:41:16.900
that's been spent in the stimulus packages

00:41:16.900 --> 00:41:19.146
just over the past few months.

00:41:19.146 --> 00:41:20.999
- And to loop back to the main topic,

00:41:20.999 --> 00:41:23.172
I mean, where is broadband
access a problem?

00:41:23.172 --> 00:41:25.420
It's much more a problem
among lower income people

00:41:25.420 --> 00:41:27.070
than upper income people.

00:41:27.070 --> 00:41:29.490
- Yeah, one question, this is coming

00:41:29.490 --> 00:41:32.480
from, it's going back to
one of your other levers

00:41:32.480 --> 00:41:33.313
on the education.

00:41:33.313 --> 00:41:35.610
I do wanna ask this 'cause
it's a thought experiment

00:41:35.610 --> 00:41:37.110
that I've had with friends, you know,

00:41:37.110 --> 00:41:38.800
just thinking about the issue.

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:41.057
Jenny Scoball from Facebook asks,

00:41:41.057 --> 00:41:43.077
"But let's say everyone
gets a college education.

00:41:43.077 --> 00:41:45.990
"Will there really be jobs
for all of those people?"

00:41:45.990 --> 00:41:46.850
I mean you could imagine,

00:41:46.850 --> 00:41:48.700
what if everyone had a PhD tomorrow?

00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:50.550
Will there really be that many jobs

00:41:50.550 --> 00:41:52.670
for those folks?

00:41:52.670 --> 00:41:53.630
- So I love this question.

00:41:53.630 --> 00:41:55.500
I get the version of this question a lot

00:41:55.500 --> 00:41:56.580
when I'm out speaking.

00:41:56.580 --> 00:41:58.330
I would actually ask people to flip it

00:41:58.330 --> 00:41:59.840
and go not forwards in time,

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:01.220
but backwards in time.

00:42:01.220 --> 00:42:02.760
Why is it necessary that everyone

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:04.070
gets a high school education?

00:42:04.070 --> 00:42:05.920
Why is it necessary that everyone gets

00:42:05.920 --> 00:42:07.310
an eighth grade education?

00:42:07.310 --> 00:42:11.490
Why did we as a society
decide that 13 years

00:42:11.490 --> 00:42:13.970
of public education was
essentially the minimum

00:42:13.970 --> 00:42:15.420
that all people should have?

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:17.810
And that's not just a
hypothetical question.

00:42:17.810 --> 00:42:21.560
There was a real, live
debate in this country

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:23.130
in the early 20th century,

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:25.200
and around the world about this.

00:42:25.200 --> 00:42:27.007
And there were people who said,

00:42:27.007 --> 00:42:29.567
"Come on, let's be realistic.

00:42:29.567 --> 00:42:34.317
"Most Americans don't
need to go to high school.

00:42:34.317 --> 00:42:37.157
"They need to learn to
read and that's about it.

00:42:37.157 --> 00:42:41.157
"The high school should be for
the top echelons of society.

00:42:41.157 --> 00:42:44.937
"It's a waste of time and
money to send ordinary workers

00:42:44.937 --> 00:42:46.190
"to high school."

00:42:46.190 --> 00:42:48.690
And then there were people,
the high school movement

00:42:48.690 --> 00:42:50.920
which was a national movement,

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:53.220
a sort of national, local movement.

00:42:53.220 --> 00:42:55.410
Maybe strongest at first in Iowa,

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:57.777
who said, "No, no, no,
education will pay off.

00:42:57.777 --> 00:42:59.987
"If we create more educated workers,

00:42:59.987 --> 00:43:01.577
"more educated citizens,

00:43:01.577 --> 00:43:03.477
"our society will be healthier.

00:43:03.477 --> 00:43:05.397
"And there will end up being jobs

00:43:05.397 --> 00:43:06.900
"for many of those people."

00:43:06.900 --> 00:43:11.230
And that second group really
won the bet in a route.

00:43:11.230 --> 00:43:15.080
Europe for a long time
kept high school education

00:43:15.080 --> 00:43:16.430
as more of an elite thing.

00:43:16.430 --> 00:43:18.480
We went to mass high school education,

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:22.370
and it built so many
great American industries.

00:43:22.370 --> 00:43:24.990
It built agriculture, to
have educated farmers.

00:43:24.990 --> 00:43:26.750
It built Hollywood.

00:43:26.750 --> 00:43:28.700
It build American manufacturing.

00:43:28.700 --> 00:43:31.090
It built one industry after another.

00:43:31.090 --> 00:43:35.360
And so if 13 years was the
amount of mass education

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:38.690
that made sense in the
early and mid-20th century,

00:43:38.690 --> 00:43:42.300
why is it that we think that
number wouldn't have gone up

00:43:42.300 --> 00:43:43.950
almost 100 years later?

00:43:43.950 --> 00:43:45.900
And I think clearly, when you think about

00:43:45.900 --> 00:43:48.110
how much more advance technology is,

00:43:48.110 --> 00:43:50.210
that number has gone up.

00:43:50.210 --> 00:43:53.410
And so thinking about 15 or 17 years

00:43:53.410 --> 00:43:56.740
as the right number for
mass education makes sense.

00:43:56.740 --> 00:43:58.840
And my instinct is if we got there,

00:43:58.840 --> 00:44:00.610
much has happened 100 years ago,

00:44:00.610 --> 00:44:02.130
we would find all kinds of job

00:44:02.130 --> 00:44:05.820
we didn't even know existed for
our more educated citizenry.

00:44:05.820 --> 00:44:08.597
And I don't think there's
a natural limit that says,

00:44:08.597 --> 00:44:10.897
"Well, nine wasn't enough education.

00:44:10.897 --> 00:44:13.587
"13 years was enough education,

00:44:13.587 --> 00:44:16.287
"but anything more than 13
would end up being wasted."

00:44:17.430 --> 00:44:19.240
- No, and obviously you're
preaching to the choir

00:44:19.240 --> 00:44:21.060
when you're talking to me about it.

00:44:21.060 --> 00:44:22.760
And the way I think about it is,

00:44:22.760 --> 00:44:25.610
there is some logic to the
argument that Jenny is making,

00:44:25.610 --> 00:44:28.330
that a degree doesn't
automatically convert into a job.

00:44:28.330 --> 00:44:30.810
But from a societal scale,

00:44:30.810 --> 00:44:32.990
and we use years as a proxy,

00:44:32.990 --> 00:44:34.630
and we use high school
and college as a proxy.

00:44:34.630 --> 00:44:36.790
But it's really can we
get people to that level

00:44:36.790 --> 00:44:37.660
of critical thinking?

00:44:37.660 --> 00:44:39.210
Can we get them to that level of skills

00:44:39.210 --> 00:44:41.560
so they can leverage the
technology around them,

00:44:41.560 --> 00:44:44.270
leverage, understand the
complexity of the environments

00:44:44.270 --> 00:44:45.750
so they can either plug in to jobs

00:44:45.750 --> 00:44:48.110
that already exist, or
will exist in 10 years.

00:44:48.110 --> 00:44:49.310
Or even more interesting,

00:44:49.310 --> 00:44:50.610
so they can create new jobs.

00:44:50.610 --> 00:44:53.180
They can be entrepreneurs
at a small or a large scale

00:44:53.180 --> 00:44:55.730
which is a job creation engine.

00:44:55.730 --> 00:44:58.610
So it is an interesting,
I think on the margin

00:44:58.610 --> 00:45:01.126
that argument, there is some.

00:45:01.126 --> 00:45:03.700
Just the piece of paper by itself

00:45:03.700 --> 00:45:04.920
isn't going to give you the job.

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:08.210
It's the fact that
you've actually developed

00:45:08.210 --> 00:45:09.043
those types of skills.

00:45:09.043 --> 00:45:09.876
And you know, we talk a lot

00:45:09.876 --> 00:45:11.610
about this labor pyramid inverting now

00:45:11.610 --> 00:45:13.550
where we need to get either people

00:45:13.550 --> 00:45:14.780
to the top of that labor pyramid

00:45:14.780 --> 00:45:16.660
into the knowledge economy
to get those skills,

00:45:16.660 --> 00:45:20.390
or the inequality's gonna keep going.

00:45:20.390 --> 00:45:21.770
So we're way over time.

00:45:21.770 --> 00:45:24.970
This was a super, I feel
like we should continue.

00:45:24.970 --> 00:45:25.803
This is part one.

00:45:25.803 --> 00:45:27.306
I'm just commit you already.
- I'd love that.

00:45:27.306 --> 00:45:29.560
(laughing)

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:30.790
- But thank you so much, David.

00:45:30.790 --> 00:45:32.730
This was a really, really
stimulating conversation,

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:35.010
a conversation that I think people

00:45:35.010 --> 00:45:38.120
need to be having more and be
having in kind of a, you know,

00:45:38.120 --> 00:45:39.700
not politicizing it.

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:40.950
Just like, what are the facts?

00:45:40.950 --> 00:45:42.050
What do we understand?

00:45:42.050 --> 00:45:43.360
What works, what doesn't?

00:45:43.360 --> 00:45:45.350
And nothing is all bad or all good,

00:45:45.350 --> 00:45:46.780
but we should all be informed.

00:45:46.780 --> 00:45:49.285
So thank you so much for participating.

00:45:49.285 --> 00:45:50.118
- Thanks for having me,

00:45:50.118 --> 00:45:51.670
and thanks to all of the viewers

00:45:51.670 --> 00:45:53.617
for the great questions.

00:45:53.617 --> 00:45:55.490
